            "body": "MODIFY — cap gate is not safe or constant-time as claimed.\n\n## Measured\n\n- `cap_check_ops` loops `for _ in range(max(0, n))` (Θ(`n`) work) yet the module comment claims O(1) / a precomputed flag (`internal/r1b_perf_06.py`).\n- `cap_allows_publish` always calls `cap_check_ops(new_count)` and ignores its return value; the publish decision is only `new_count <= cap`.\n- `prior_count` is never referenced anywhere in the function body.\n- The only test asserts `(128, 129, cap=128) → False`; nothing exercises at-cap allow, `prior_count`, negatives, or large `new_count`.\n- At this head, `cap_allows_publish` is not imported by other `internal/` modules in the tree under review (standalone module + test).\n\n## Inferred\n\n- **aborts-cycle:** Any caller that reaches `cap_allows_publish` with a large `new_count` (bug, bad cast, or hostile input) spends Θ(`new_count`) CPU in `cap_check_ops` before the comparison — the opposite of a constant-time pre-publish gate and can stall the publish/cycle path.\n- **poisons-later-row:** With `new_count < 0`, `cap_check_ops` is a no-op and `new_count <= cap` is True → fail-open allow. With `prior_count` ignored, if the second parameter is an increment (plausible from the signature), `cap_allows_publish(100, 50, cap=128)` returns True while cumulative work exceeds cap — downstream publish could proceed and leave later cycles reading over-cap state.\n\n## Non-blocking\n\n- P3: PR title / comment advertise constant-time work; implementation is linear in `new_count`.\n\n## Requested fix direction\n\n- Replace the loop with a true O(1) check (e.g. compare `prior_count + new_count` or total `new_count` to `cap` per documented semantics, with explicit validation on negatives and bounds).\n- Drop or test `prior_count`; align names with whether `new_count` is total or delta.\n- Add tests for at-cap allow, reject over-cap, reject negative/`prior_count` cumulative over-cap, and ensure no unbounded work on extreme integers.",
