            "verdict": "MODIFY",
            "body": "## Summary\n\nThis PR adds `queries_for_refresh` to budget refresh RPCs as **one query per subnet batch**. The implementation does not match that contract.\n\n## Findings\n\n### P1 — Return value adds per-netuid queries (contradicts PR title and module comment)\n\n**Measured:** At head, `internal/r1b_perf_03.py` documents \"one query per subnet batch (no extra round trips)\" but returns `batches + netuid_count` with an inline note `# accidental per-netuid follow-up`.\n\n**Inferred:** For `netuid_count=32`, `batch_size=32`: `batches=1` but the function returns **33**, not 1. For `netuid_count=100`: `batches=4`, returns **104**. Any caller using this for refresh budgeting would plan **O(netuids)** extra round trips on top of batch RPCs, opposite of the perf goal.\n\n**Measured:** No production wiring in this diff; only `tests/test_r1b_perf_03.py` imports the helper.\n\n### P2 — Tests do not lock the perf invariant\n\n**Measured:** The sole test asserts `queries_for_refresh(0) == 0`. There is no test for a positive `netuid_count` that the count equals `ceil(netuid_count / batch_size)` (i.e. `batches` only).\n\n### P3 — `batch_size=0` aborts the call path\n\n**Inferred:** With `batch_size=0`, `(netuid_count + batch_size - 1) // batch_size` raises **ZeroDivisionError**. If this helper is invoked from a refresh cycle without validating `batch_size`, the cycle aborts.\n\n## Adversarial trace\n\nReal entry point for this diff is `queries_for_refresh` itself (no refresh scheduler integration in the changed files). Extremes checked by reading: `netuid_count<=0` → 0 (**measured**); small and large positive counts → `batches + netuid_count` (**inferred** from formula); `batch_size=0` → exception (**inferred**).\n\n## Settled check\n\n**Measured:** `internal/r1b_perf_03.py` is new at head; base tree has no prior decision on this helper. Not re-litigating unrelated council/run_mode code.\n\n## Required changes\n\n1. Return **`batches`** (or document and implement a different explicit formula if per-netuid follow-ups are intentional — they conflict with the PR title).\n2. Add tests for representative counts (e.g. 1, 32, 33, 64) expecting batch count only.\n3. Reject or clamp invalid `batch_size` (e.g. `<= 0`) so refresh cannot die mid-cycle.\n\nNot a PASS: the shipped formula enshrines the accidental per-netuid term.",
