            "body": "## Summary\n\nHead `fa309e6be3cd6b96b564034999a0dc9158fe867e` adds `internal/r1b_perf_06.py` and one pytest. The PR title promises a **constant-time cap check before publishing**; the code does the opposite and leaves cap semantics incomplete.\n\n## Findings\n\n### P1 — Linear-time work on the cap gate (mislabeled O(1))\n\n**Measured:** `cap_allows_publish` always calls `cap_check_ops(new_count)`, which runs `for _ in range(max(0, n))` and increments a counter — **O(n)** in `new_count`, not O(1). The module comment on line 2 claims \"O(1) using a precomputed flag,\" which is false for the shipped code.\n\n**Inferred:** If this helper is placed on a publish or scoring hot path (as the title implies), large or attacker-influenced `new_count` values (e.g. `10**9`, `2**63`) can **stall or abort a cycle** via unbounded iteration or `OverflowError`/`MemoryError` from `range`, instead of a bounded comparison.\n\n**Blocking:** `aborts-cycle`\n\n### P2 — `prior_count` is unused\n\n**Measured:** `cap_allows_publish(prior_count, new_count, *, cap=128)` never reads `prior_count`; it returns only `new_count <= cap`.\n\n**Inferred:** If `new_count` is an increment and the cap applies to cumulative size (typical for \"growth\" tests), calls like `cap_allows_publish(200, 10, cap=128)` return **True** while total exposure is 210 — a fail-open cap bug once wired.\n\n### P3 — No production wiring yet\n\n**Measured:** At head, `list_repo_files` shows only `internal/r1b_perf_06.py`, tests, `snapshot_coverage.py`, and `run_mode.py`. Nothing imports `cap_allows_publish`. Existing publish/cap policy in `snapshot_coverage.py` still gates on receipt `cap_reached` via `membership_allows_admission` — unchanged by this PR.\n\n**Inferred:** Landing this module as-is would not change production behavior today, but the API is misleading relative to the title and would be dangerous at the first call site.\n\n### P4 — Test coverage\n\n**Measured:** `test_cap_blocks_growth_beyond_limit` only asserts `(128, 129, cap=128) is False`. No allowed-path test, no `prior_count` semantics, no guard against the O(n) side effect.\n\n## Recommendation\n\nReplace the loop with a true O(1) check (define whether the cap applies to `new_count` alone or `prior_count + new_count`, document it, and implement that). Drop `cap_check_ops` from the hot path or repurpose it only in tests if you need to simulate cost. Add tests for allow/deny boundaries including `prior_count`.\n\n## Skills\n\n| Skill | Found | Changed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| adversarial-review | O(n) side effect on cap gate; unused prior_count; extremes on `range(n)` | — |\n| owner-rules | Fail-closed cap semantics elsewhere; this API would fail open on cumulative cap if wired | — |\n| settled-check | No prior repo decision covers this new module | — |",
            "skills": [
